WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:04.770
Here are the seven rules of free speech in
schools.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.800
One: Students from first grade to 12 can't
be punished based on their political or religious speech

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.600
as the Supreme Court said in the Tinker case:

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.960
'It can hardly be argued that students shed their constitutional rights at the schoolhouse gate.'

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.380
This protects, for instance, wearing black
arm bands to protest a war,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.259
distributing religious messages to classmates
between classes,

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.520
or criticizing the teacher over lunch.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.500
Two: Schools can punish speech that materially
disrupts school work,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.520
for instance, because it prompts fights.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.400
That's why lower courts have let schools ban
clothing that displays confederate flags

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.700
and in one case even clothing that displays
an American flag on Cinco de Mayo

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.820
when that display lead to violence.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.440
That's a kind of heckler's veto, where threats
against the speaker

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.969
are used by the government to justify restricting
the speaker

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:47.300
rather than the threaders.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.900
Usually the government can't impose such a
heckler's veto

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.920
but the rule in schools seems to be different.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.800
That may not seem right to you but I don't make
the law, I just make videos about it.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.700
Schools can also punish students for using
vulgarities or sexual innuendo.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.300
So if you do criticize that teacher over lunch,
be, you know, polite about it.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.460
Four: Schools can punish speech that seems
to praise drug use

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.740
and probably also alcohol use and other crimes

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.200
at least when that speech doesn't seem political.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.880
That's from Morse v. Frederick where the Supreme
Court said a school could suspend a student

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.620
for holding up a banner that read 'Bong Hits
4 Jesus.'

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.440
Yes, this is a real case.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.380
In fact you can actually find audio

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.980
of Supreme Court Justice Stephen Breyer saying
'Bong Hits 4 Jesus.'

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.479
Which is completely surreal.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.380
So, 'legalize marijuana' is probably okay

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.380
but 'marijuana is awesome' probably isn't.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.390
Five: You may have the right to speak at school

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.080
but you don't have the right to speak in the
school newspaper.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.360
Courts see the newspaper as the school's own
speech

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.520
even if the students are the ones who wrote
it.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.640
The principal has ultimate control over what
goes in the newspaper

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.440
and can delete or edit any article for pretty
much any reason.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.660
Six: Everything said so far deals with public
schools.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.340
Under the so-called state action doctrine,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.740
the First Amendment doesn't limit private
schools.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.659
Even ones that get tax breaks or government
funds.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:04.380
The first words of the first amendment are
'Congress shall make no law'

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.890
that applies to the federal government.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.060
The Fourteenth Amendment basically says no
state shall violate people's rights.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.100
That applies the First Amendment to state
and local governments.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.980
But the Constitution generally doesn't constrain
private property owners,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.480
private landlords, private schools or anyone
else.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.200
That's why when your parents told you to stop
saying rude things at the dinner table

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.080
and you asked 'what about my First Amendment
rights.'

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.760
They said 'let's talk about the state action
doctrine.'

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.220
Or at least that's what they should have said.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.340
Seven: It's all different in California.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.020
Sure everything is different in California,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.500
but what I mean is that everything so far is the
constitutional minimum that all states must provide.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.840
Some states have passed laws that provide
more protections to students.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.710
In California, for instance, high school students
generally can't be punished for their speech

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.940
even if it provokes other students into acting
disruptively.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.000
They generally can't be punished for speech
about drugs or alcohol or even vulgar speech.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.800
And public school newspapers are mostly free
from being censored by the administration.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.420
Student newspapers are protected in some other
states too.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.820
Indeed, in California, all the student speech
protections,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.060
but not the student newspaper speech protections,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.700
apply to private high school students as well.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.680
But not in religious high schools.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:07.700
So, there you have it.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.560
Political and religious speech is protected,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.500
but not disruptive speech, vulgar speech,
or speech praising drugs.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.240
Also official school newspapers are the school's
own speech,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.090
this stuff really only applies to public schools

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.960
and of course, California is different.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.580
That's the law of free speech in school,

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.960
cobbled together by the court and a few legislatures
over the past 50 years or so.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.140
This is not legal advice,

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.370
if this were legal advice, it would be followed
by a bill.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.500
Please use responsibly.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.500
Written by Eugene Volokh,

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.480
who is an actual real life First Amendment
Law Professor at UCLA.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.800
I'm Eugene Volokh and I approve this message.

